  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1218 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe ridge will gradually shift west into New Mexico on Sunday,\ndisrupting our moisture inflow. However, with weaker winds aloft,\nthis will allow for the pooling of moisture across western and \nnorthern New Mexico. This setup looks to produce the most \nwidespread coverage we will see over the next few days, favoring \nthe northern high terrain. Storm motions will be slow and erratic \nincreasing the potential for locally heavy rainfall and isolated \nflash flooding. Additionally, soils in northeast NM are incredibly\ndry and may act as hydrophobic surfaces, allowing for fast runoff\nand quickly developing flash flooding. One thing that will need \nto be watched is how Saturday's storm development and remnant \noutflow boundaries may affect the placement and strength of the \nforecast storms on Sunday. \n\nBy early in the work week, the strengthening ridge will promote \ndrier and warmer conditions, allowing highs to return to near or \nslightly above normal. Forecast confidence is still a bit low as \nwe head past midweek. However, models are starting to come into a \nbit more agreement regarding the evolution of the western ridge \nand downstream troughing. As of writing, models have a weak trough\nmoving across the Rockies and through the Plains, sending a \nbackdoor front or two through the northeastern corner of the \nregion. This will help increase precipitation chances once again\nby late in the week, however, mainly focused on the higher \nterrain. Since a good deal of uncertainty still surrounds the \nforecast this far out, details are subject to adjustment as the \nlarger scale pattern becomes better defined in the coming days.",
